  "reviewer": "Codex implementation/source review; not independent security certification",
  "scope_review": "SDK-free contract/event storage, complete pure import closure, finite independent custody model and type-negative controls. Core suites pass in Debug/ReleaseSafe; logs explicitly distinguish cached and executed steps. Seven external consumer targets compile only. Plans use borrowed explicit configuration and detect drift; they do not authorize configuration or pin certificate file contents. Input capacity is per level in the native consumer and input_capacity is distinct from event/network/local waits. The surplus-Initial failure discovered during Q00 review is fixed by per-message input leasing and rejection of queued old-level surplus before events/readiness. The same external production probe now rejects with UnexpectedLevel; both roles and native budgets 1/16384 are covered by the tenth conformance test. Unchanged core, ABI and records evidence is reused after reviewing its unchanged execution closure; the actual Engine and production consumer are re-executed in both modes. The implemented native provider is Windows x86_64 with the exact OpenSSL 3.5.8 SDK and pinned Zig. LP64/other native targets explicitly remain unavailable, with no cross-build substituted for runtime/ABI evidence. T07/T09/T13 and the QUIC endpoint/resource/independent-interop graph remain unfinished. The direct reference adapter uses the same OpenSSL provider, bypasses the new C adapter/Engine and independently encrypts a genuine QUIC short-header packet; it is not independent QUIC endpoint interoperability. Existing records sources, package pins, frozen QUIC fixture and all SDK files are preserved. ",
